On Monday, April 16, 2012 3:00:46 PM UTC-7, Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> On Apr 16, 2:02 pm, Sean Staggs <seansta...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> First I believe I am supposed to compile first, then patch right?
>
> That seems backwards to me.  Ordinarily you apply patches
> to source code.  Compilation[1] reads the (possibly patched)
> source code and produces executable code.

     The patch is applied to modify the source code, which
then needs to be compiled.  But it is recommended that you
build the unmodified source code first, to verify that your
build environment is set up and operating correctly.  If you
just apply the patch first and then fail to build, it may
be difficult to tell whether the problem is due to compiler
set up (including 'make' and Makefile, which aren't part of
the compiler at all, and might be called something completely
different if part of an integrated development environment...)
or due to broken source patch.

     In this case, the unmodified sources did build ok, so
the problem is with the patch, although it sounds like it's
with applying the patch rather than with the changes made
to the sources by the patch.


